The program reads up to 100 integers with [38;5;10mscanf[0m, then replaces each with [38;5;10mx*x + x[0m in place and prints one per line. Three C behaviors needed deliberate emulation rather than idiomatic Rust:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m tokenizing.** Implemented as a byte scanner over stdin that skips all C [38;5;10misspace[0m characters (including [38;5;10m\n[0m, [38;5;10m\v[0m, [38;5;10m\f[0m, [38;5;10m\r[0m), accepts an optional sign, then decimal digits. A token like [38;5;10mabc[0m, [38;5;10m1.5[0m, or a bare [38;5;10m-[0m stops the loop exactly where C's [38;5;10m!= 1[0m check does, and [38;5;10mi[0m retains the count of values read before the failure — so [38;5;10m0x10[0m yields [38;5;10m0[0m then stops, matching C.[0m[0m
- **Integer overflow on input.** glibc's [38;5;10m%d[0m runs [38;5;10mstrtol[0m, which saturates to the [38;5;10mlong[0m range, then stores into an [38;5;10mint[0m, truncating. So [38;5;10m99999999999[0m becomes [38;5;10m-1[0m in C, not a clamp to [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m. [38;5;10msaturate_then_truncate[0m reproduces that two-step path; digit accumulation uses [38;5;10mi128[0m with a guard so arbitrarily long digit runs can't panic.[0m[0m
- **Aliased FMA.** [38;5;10mfma_array(out, out, out, out, len)[0m passes one buffer as all four parameters, so each element is computed from its own current value. That's expressed as a single in-place slice transform using [38;5;10mwrapping_mul[0m/[38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m — C signed overflow is UB, but the compiled code wraps, and inputs like [38;5;10m46341[0m and [38;5;10m2147483647[0m confirm identical results.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Output goes through a [38;5;10mBufWriter[0m with an explicit flush, mirroring stdio buffering. Everything is safe Rust; no [38;5;10munsafe[0m blocks. One intentional deviation: stdin is slurped up front rather than read incrementally, which is unobservable here since nothing else consumes the stream.[0m[0m
